#!/usr/bin/env bash
# heartbeat-check — the OTHER half of the missed-run alarm. Runs on a DIFFERENT HOST's crond
# (server3) and reads memo-watch's heartbeat off NFS.
#
# ⛔ WHY IT CANNOT LIVE ON server4 WITH THE JOB IT WATCHES. A job that is not running cannot
#    report that it is not running — and that is the exact failure this whole loop was
#    corrected for on 2026-09-08, when the broker fired 798-810 and NOT ONE reached the seat:
#    6.5 hours of no monitoring produced output byte-identical to 13 clean windows. The watch's
#    own run-gap check (step 0) sees a gap only AFTER it ends, on the next run that happens.
#    This sees a gap WHILE IT IS OPEN, and it survives server4's crond dying, its crontab being
#    edited, and the host being down.
# ⚠️ WHAT IT STILL CANNOT SEE, stated rather than hidden: it shares the NFS mount and the ATC
#    broker with the thing it watches. NAS down or broker down ⇒ it alarms (correct direction).
#    server3 down ⇒ nothing watches the watch, and nothing tells you. That residual is real.
#
# EXIT CONTRACT (same convention as `run`, declared in cronguard-digest.conf):
#   0 = heartbeat fresh      -> silent
#   1 = heartbeat STALE      -> DMs `memo`. rc1 = findings.
#   2 = heartbeat UNREADABLE -> DMs `memo`. rc2 = unknown. Absence is NOT freshness.
set -u
export PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# ⛔ AN INTERMITTENT TIMING FAULT LOGGED WITHOUT A TIMESTAMP CANNOT BE ASKED "WHEN".
#    1,492 log lines held 10 UNREADABLE events and NOT ONE carried a clock, so the one
#    question the open investigation needs -- do these cluster in a particular minute of
#    the hour (memo 3284310d, the ":20-slot" confound) -- could not be answered from the
#    record at all, only guessed at from the two alerts a reader happened to see arrive.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE ANSWER WAS ALREADY ON DISK, IN A DIFFERENT LOG, THE WHOLE TIME.
#    Adding this stamp() was right but the conclusion drawn beside it -- "so the series
#    starts 2026-10-23, this is NOT retroactive" -- was WRONG, and it was wrong because
#    only the job's OWN log was searched. cronguard writes every non-completion to
#    /tmp/cronguard-misses.log WITH an ISO timestamp, going back to 2026-10-03.
#    ⚠️ A TIMESTAMP THIS JOB NEVER WROTE WAS BEING WRITTEN FOR IT BY ITS WRAPPER.
#    ⇒ Before declaring a question un-answerable from the record, enumerate every log
#      that observes the job, not just the one it writes itself.
#
# ⭐ MEASURED 2026-10-23 from that log, and the clustering is not subtle:
#    the job's own UNREADABLE exit (rc=2) has fired 11 times and ALL 11 were at :20.
#    ZERO at :00, ZERO at :40. The same log's 4 rc=1 (STALE) events are at :40/:40/:00/:00
#    -- a negative control from the same instrument, so this is not a logging artefact.
#    DEFERRED (host io pressure, job never ran) is uniform 19/19/16 across the three
#    slots, so the slots fire equally often and :20 is not over-sampled.
#    First rc=2: 2026-10-22T13:20. The fault is ~24h old, NOT the ~32h estimated by
#    counting log lines -- that estimate assumed no run was ever skipped, and 54 were.
#
# ⛔ TWO CANDIDATE CAUSES SURVIVE THIS, AND THE :20 FIGURE DOES NOT SEPARATE THEM.
#    Writer cron is 11,41; reader cron WAS 0,20,40. So:
#      (a) ATTRIBUTE CACHE. :20 reads a heartbeat ~540s old -- the ONLY slot inside the
#          fleet's 600s NFS acattrmax. :00 reads ~1140s, :40 ~1740s. The logged "fresh:"
#          ages confirm those figures exactly.
#      (b) CONTENTION. server4 and server5 both run 5,20,35,50 jobs. A reader sampling
#          only 0/20/40 can overlap a 5,20,35,50 job at :20 and nowhere else.
#    ⇒ BOTH predict exactly 11/11 at :20. Neither is evidence over the other.
#
# ⭐ EXPERIMENT ARMED 2026-10-23 13:22, PREDICTIONS RECORDED BEFORE THE RESULT:
#    reader cron moved 0,20,40 -> 5,25,45 on server3. Same 3 runs/hour, no coverage lost.
#      (a) attribute cache  => failures move to :45 ONLY (reads the :41 write, ~240s old;
#                              :05 reads :41 at ~1440s, :25 reads :11 at ~840s -- both out)
#      (b) contention       => failures move to :05 ONLY (the 5,20,35,50 overlap)
#    The two predicted slots are DISJOINT, so the next few rc=2 events decide it.
#    ⚠️ If failures stay at :20 with nothing scheduled there, BOTH are wrong.
#    ⛔ DO NOT revert this schedule until the question is settled -- reverting it
#      destroys the only discriminator, and the fault is intermittent enough that
#      re-arming costs another day.
#
# ⭐⭐ RESULT 2026-10-23 18:13, AND BOTH PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS ARE REFUTED.
#    On 5,25,45 the reader ran 15 times over 5 hours (13:25 -> 18:05): ZERO failures.
#      - prediction (a) attribute cache said :45 would fail. It did not, 5/5, and it
#        reads the FRESHEST heartbeat of any slot (~220-233s, well inside 600s) --
#        fresher than the ~540s that was failing. THE CACHE STORY IS DEAD.
#      - prediction (b) contention said :05 would fail. It did not, 5/5, and :05 IS
#        one of the 5,20,35,50 slots it blamed. THE CONTENTION STORY IS DEAD.
#    ⭐ Writing both predictions down first is what makes this a result instead of a
#      shrug: neither survived, and neither can now be quietly re-fitted to the data.
#
# ⛔⛔ BUT DO NOT READ "0 FAILURES" AS "MOVING THE SLOT FIXED IT". I CONFOUNDED THIS.
#    The last failure was 13:20:01 and I changed the schedule at 13:22 -- two minutes
#    later. So "moving off :20 avoided the fault" and "the fault stopped on its own at
#    13:20" predict EXACTLY the same 5 hours of silence, and nothing here separates
#    them. ⚠️ An intervention made immediately after the last occurrence of an
#    intermittent fault cannot be credited with its absence. a31 also resumed at 16:05
#    and volume1 noatime is on, so the NAS was not held still either.
#
# ⭐ REVERSAL ARMED 2026-10-23 18:13: cron 5,25,45 -> 5,20,25,45. :20 is restored
#    ALONGSIDE the three controls, so slot and time are separated WITHIN each hour:
#      :20 fails while :05/:25/:45 do not  => the slot is causal and the fault is live
#      nothing fails at all                => the fault ended around 13:20, and every
#                                             slot-based reading of it was noise
#      everything fails                    => it was never about the slot
#    ⚠️ The third outcome is the one that would have been invisible on 0,20,40:
#      with only one sampled slot inside each 20-minute span there was no within-hour
#      control, so a fault that was really about TIME read as a fault about SLOT.
#
# ⭐ INTERIM 2026-10-23 22:10, AND THE STOPPING RULE IS WRITTEN HERE BEFORE THE REST OF
#    THE DATA ARRIVES -- which is the only thing that will stop it being re-fitted later.
#    Since the reversal: 17 runs, 18:05 -> 22:05, ZERO failures and ZERO misses.
#    :20 ran 4 times (18:20 19:20 20:20 21:20) and passed 4/4; :05 :25 :45 also 4/4.
#    ⛔ THAT IS NOT YET OUTCOME 2, AND THE TEMPTATION TO CALL IT IS THE WHOLE PROBLEM.
#      While the fault was live, 11 of the 25 :20-slots from 2026-10-22T13:20 to
#      2026-10-23T13:20 failed, p = 0.44. FOUR clean :20 runs happen by luck with
#      probability 0.56^4 = 0.098 even if the fault is FULLY live.
#      ⇒ A ONE-IN-TEN COINCIDENCE IS NOT A REFUTATION.
#    ⭐ PRE-REGISTERED: declare "the fault ended ~13:20" only at >= 12 CLEAN :20 RUNS
#      (0.56^12 = 9.5e-4), reached around 06:20 on 2026-10-24. A SINGLE rc=2 at :20
#      with the three controls clean ends it earlier and the other way -- outcome 1.
#    ⚠️ THE DENOMINATOR IS AN ASSUMPTION AND I AM NAMING IT, BECAUSE THE LAST FIGURE I
#      BUILT THIS WAY WAS WRONG. "25 slots" assumes cron fired at every :20 in that
#      window. cronguard logs ONLY non-completions -- 11 at :20, all rc=2, zero DEFERRED
#      and zero rc=99 -- so it can prove no run was deferred or lock-skipped, and it
#      CANNOT prove cron fired at all. The job's own log cannot settle it either: it
#      carried NO timestamps until stamp() was added at 13:20 that same day, which is
#      why it holds exactly one :20 line from the entire fault window.
#      ⇒ If any :20 slot silently did not fire, the true p is HIGHER and 4 clean runs
#        are MORE surprising, not less -- the assumption errs against my own conclusion,
#        which is the only direction in which an unverifiable denominator is safe.
#    ⚠️ AND THE FRESHNESS NUMBERS ALREADY SAY SOMETHING, FROM INSIDE THE SAME HOURS AS
#      THE CONTROLS: :20 still reads a 475-518s heartbeat, still the ONLY slot inside
#      the 600s acattrmax, and it is not failing. The cache story was already dead on
#      the 5,25,45 data; it is now dead without the time-of-day confound that killed it.
# ⭐⭐ VERDICT 2026-10-24 06:25 -- OUTCOME 2, AT THE PRE-REGISTERED THRESHOLD, NOT RE-FITTED.
#    13 clean :20 runs since the reversal armed 2026-10-23T18:13 -- 18:20 19:20 20:20
#    21:20 22:20 23:20 00:20 01:20 02:20 03:20 04:20 05:20 06:20, every one "fresh"
#    (475-533s). The rule said >= 12; 0.56^13 = 5.3e-4.
#    ZERO rc=2 anywhere since arming: the newest rc=2 in /tmp/cronguard-misses.log is
#    2026-10-23T13:20:01, five hours BEFORE the reversal. Controls :05 :25 :45 are clean
#    across the same 12.2 hours, so outcome 3 (failures at every slot) is excluded too.
#    ⇒ THE FAULT ENDED AROUND 2026-10-23 13:20, AND EVERY SLOT-BASED READING OF IT WAS
#      NOISE -- including the 11-of-11-at-:20 clustering that opened this investigation.
#
# ⛔ WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY. It does not say what the fault WAS, and it does not say that
#    anything stopped it. 13:20 is the last rc=2 in the observed series, an endpoint, not
#    a demonstrated cause. a31 resumed 16:05 and volume1 noatime was already on, so the
#    NAS was not held still. "What made the heartbeat unreadable 11 times in 24h" is
#    CLOSED AS UNEXPLAINED, not solved. If rc=2 returns, re-open this verdict first.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE METHOD IS THE DURABLE PART, AND IT COST THREE DEAD HYPOTHESES TO LEARN IT.
#    An 11-of-11 clustering in a single slot, with a clean negative control from the same
#    instrument (4 rc=1 at :40/:40/:00/:00) and equal sampling across slots, looked like
#    strong evidence -- and was noise. What exposed it was not a better theory. It was
#    restoring the SUSPECT slot ALONGSIDE its controls so that slot and time were
#    separated WITHIN each hour, and writing the stopping rule down BEFORE the data
#    arrived. Both earlier interventions were confounded the same way: each was made
#    immediately after the last occurrence of an intermittent fault, so "the fix worked"
#    and "it stopped on its own" predicted identical silence.
#    ⛔ Do not revive the attribute-cache or contention stories. Both are refuted above,
#      and :20 is STILL the only slot inside the 600s acattrmax while passing 13/13.
#
# ⭐ THE SCHEDULE STAYS 5,20,25,45. It is no longer a discriminator, but it costs nothing,
#    it keeps the within-hour control that made this readable at all, and reverting it
#    restores the 0,20,40 sampling that let a fault about TIME read as a fault about SLOT.

stamp() { date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z'; }

HB=${MEMO_WATCH_HEARTBEAT:-/mnt/nas/data/memo-watch/heartbeat}
BROKER=${ATC_BROKER:-http://192.168.1.168:3030}
DM_TO=${MEMO_WATCH_DM_TO:-memo}
# ⛔⛔ DERIVE THIS, DO NOT PICK IT — AND RE-DERIVE IT WHEN THE UPSTREAM GUARD CHANGES.
#    History, because both wrong values were wrong for instructive reasons:
#    4200 ORIGINALLY, justified as "two missed 30-min windows plus slack" — an unexamined guess.
#    6300 BRIEFLY on 2026-10-04 02:43, after a real 90-min gap. That derivation used cronguard's
#      600s TIMEOUT as the run-duration term. ⛔ WRONG INPUT: a timeout is an upper bound on a hang,
#      not a duration. MEASURED the same morning over 3 control runs: 6.7s, 6.9s, 7.8s. Using the
#      timeout over-provisioned the threshold by ~45 min of detection latency.
#    ⇒ BACK TO 4200, re-derived against the FIXED guard:
#      cronguard 355a44e (2026-10-04, `scripts` seat) now rounds ELAPSED to the nearest minute before
#      the ceiling comparison (cronguard:468, feeding the decision at :470), so the one-second jitter
#      miss that produced the 90-min gap cannot recur. Worst legitimate gap is back to
#      cadence + ceiling-at-next-fire = 30 + 30 = 60 min, plus ~8s of run duration and ~1s of cron
#      jitter => ~3608s. 4200 leaves ~10 min of margin, and the observer error that used to inflate
#      this (a 600s-stale NFS attribute cache) is removed by the forced revalidation below.
#    ⭐ THE RULE: a staleness threshold is a FUNCTION of the upstream guard's worst legitimate gap.
#      When that guard is fixed, the threshold must come DOWN again — otherwise every upstream bug
#      permanently buys itself detection latency that nobody ever reclaims.
#    ⛔ Do not raise this to buy margin against a bug that has been fixed. Re-measure instead:
#      the defer pattern is in /tmp/cronguard-decisions.log on server4 (grep memo-watch).
STALE_S=${MEMO_WATCH_STALE_S:-4200}     # 70 min; derived above against cronguard >= 355a44e.
TMP=$(mktemp -d)
trap 'rm -rf "$TMP"' EXIT

dm() {  # dm <subject> <body>
  python3 - "$DM_TO" "$1" "$2" > "$TMP/dm.json" <<'PY'
import json,sys
print(json.dumps({"to":sys.argv[1],"from":"memo","durable":True,
                  "subject":sys.argv[2],"content":sys.argv[3]}))
PY
  # ⛔⛔ DECIDE ON THE HTTP CODE, NEVER ON curl's EXIT STATUS, AND NEVER ON NEITHER.
  #    The previous form here printed `DM HTTP=%{http_code}` and then `|| true` — it reported the
  #    code and decided on NOTHING, so a 404 printed "DM HTTP=404" and the script carried on exiting
  #    1 exactly as if the alarm had been delivered. A dead route is a 404 and curl still exits 0.
  #    This is the defect that silently killed scripts/atc-say-ben for a day; it was fixed in this
  #    loop's OTHER half (scripts/memo-watch/run) on 2026-10-02 and NOT carried across until now.
  #    ⇒ When you fix a defect in one half of a two-host loop, fix the other half in the same pass.
  if [ -n "${MEMO_WATCH_NO_DM:-}" ]; then
    echo "  ⚠️ MEMO_WATCH_NO_DM set -- DM SUPPRESSED (control run). Payload that would have been sent:"
    sed 's/^/     /' "$TMP/dm.json" | head -3
    # ⭐ A CONTROL RUN MUST STILL EXERCISE THE SEND PATH, or it is a dry-run/real-send split that
    #    masks a dead route. Suppress the DELIVERY, never the RESOLUTION.
    RC_PROBE=$(curl -sS --max-time 15 -X POST "$BROKER/messages" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
                    -d '{}' -o "$TMP/route-probe.json" -w '%{http_code}' 2>/dev/null)
    if [ "$RC_PROBE" = "400" ] && command grep -q 'required' "$TMP/route-probe.json" 2>/dev/null; then
      echo "  ✅ send path VERIFIED: POST $BROKER/messages resolves (400 from the handler, not 404)"
    else
      echo "  ⛔ SEND PATH IS DEAD OR CHANGED: POST $BROKER/messages -> HTTP ${RC_PROBE:-<none>}"
      echo "     ⇒ A REAL finding would NOT have reached the seat. Fix the route before trusting silence."
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  DM_CODE=$(curl -sS --max-time 15 -X POST "$BROKER/messages" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
                 --data-binary @"$TMP/dm.json" -o "$TMP/dm-resp.json" -w '%{http_code}' 2>/dev/null)
  echo "  DM HTTP=${DM_CODE:-<none>}"
  case "${DM_CODE:-}" in
    2*) if command grep -q '"sent":0' "$TMP/dm-resp.json" 2>/dev/null; then
          echo "  ⛔ DM ACCEPTED BUT NOT ROUTED (sent:0) -- the finding stands and reached nobody."
          head -c 300 "$TMP/dm-resp.json"
        else
          echo "  ✅ DM accepted: $(head -c 200 "$TMP/dm-resp.json" 2>/dev/null)"
        fi ;;
    *)  echo "  ⛔ DM SEND FAILED (HTTP ${DM_CODE:-<none>}) -- the finding stands; it did not reach the seat."
        head -c 300 "$TMP/dm-resp.json" 2>/dev/null
        command grep -q 'no_such_route' "$TMP/dm-resp.json" 2>/dev/null \
          && echo "  ⛔⛔ no_such_route: THE ALARM PATH ITSELF IS BROKEN, not this one message." ;;
  esac
}

# ⛔⛔ AN UNREADABLE HEARTBEAT HAS TWO CAUSES AND THEY NEED DIFFERENT PEOPLE.
#    "the watch is dead" is memo's problem; "/mnt/nas is not answering" is the
#    NAS's. This alarm used to name both and settle neither, so every firing
#    cost a human the same investigation. It fired three times on 2026-10-22
#    and the answer was identical each time.
#    ⇒ RUN A CONTROL BEFORE DECIDING. A static file in the SAME MOUNT separates
#      the two: if the control answers fast while the heartbeat does not, the
#      mount is healthy and the contention is on that one file.
#    ⭐ MEASURED 2026-10-22 15:20 from server3, which is why this exists:
#      heartbeat stat 9,160ms · a static file in the same mount 92ms · the mount
#      root 12ms. The NAS was fine. The heartbeat is rewritten every cadence, and
#      it is the REWRITING that makes it slow to stat, not the filesystem.
mount_control() {   # echoes "<ms> <rc>" for a static path in the same mount
  # ⛔ `stat`, NOT `test -f`. The first version of this used `test -f` for both
  #    probes and the mount ROOT is a DIRECTORY, so a perfectly healthy mount
  #    answering in 2ms returned rc=1 and the alarm declared an NFS outage.
  #    Caught by this script's own negative control on the first run, which is
  #    the whole reason to write one: a control that cannot fail proves nothing,
  #    and this one failed usefully before it ever reached a real alert.
  # ⛔ THE CONTROL MUST BE DERIVED FROM $HB, NEVER HARDCODED. The first version
  #    probed /mnt/nas whatever $HB was, so pointing the check at a dead mount
  #    still reported "the mount is healthy" -- it vouched for a mount it had
  #    not tested. Caught by control B below, which is the one that is supposed
  #    to catch it.
  local p="$1"
  local s e rc
  s=$(date +%s%N); timeout 10 stat -c %Y "$p" >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?; e=$(date +%s%N)
  echo "$(( (e - s) / 1000000 )) $rc"
}

# /mnt/nas/data/memo-watch/heartbeat -> /mnt/nas : the mount $HB actually lives on.
hb_mount_root() { printf '/%s/%s' "$(echo "$HB" | cut -d/ -f2)" "$(echo "$HB" | cut -d/ -f3)"; }

# ⛔⛔ A DIRECTORY IS NOT A STATIC FILE, AND UNTIL 2026-10-22 17:30 THIS CHECK PROBED THE
#    DIRECTORY WHILE ITS OWN COMMENT CLAIMED "a static file in the SAME MOUNT". The anchor
#    measurement it cites (92ms) was taken on a static FILE, /mnt/nas/data/code/memo/README.md.
#    The two cannot answer the same question: a slow heartbeat beside a fast DIRECTORY says
#    the mount is up, but a slow heartbeat beside a fast static FILE IN THE SAME DIRECTORY is
#    what isolates the fault to that one file. Both are now probed, and they give different
#    verdicts -- see hb_static_control.
# ⭐ WHY THE DISTINCTION EARNS ITS KEEP, measured the same day by nfs-op-class-probe:
#    directory-mutating operations are the expensive class on this mount -- paired within a
#    round across six hosts, CREATE stalled where the SETATTR two seconds beside it did not
#    in 165 rounds against 41 (p<0.0001), taking 35.8% of the window's wall clock against
#    2.5% -- and `run` replaces the heartbeat by atomic rename every cadence, which IS a
#    directory mutation. So "this directory is being churned" is a live rival to "this file
#    is hot", and only a static sibling can tell them apart.
# ⛔ CITE THE PAIRED COLUMNS, NOT THE p50s. This comment used to quote 28,470ms against
#    472ms from the fixed-order run, where every op owned one position and position and
#    operation were the same measurement; and a p50 on this mount is a statement about the
#    hour it was taken in -- the same probe read SETATTR at 510ms, 11ms and 112ms in three
#    windows on one afternoon. The CREATE direction replicated across both rotated runs at
#    p<0.0001; the magnitudes never transferred, and neither did any effect weaker than that
#    (two at p=0.003-0.05 failed to replicate, one flipping direction). Figures and the
#    retraction: nfs-op-class-results.md.
# ⚠️ The directory-slow threshold is a VARIABLE so the control harness can force that branch.
#    A verdict no test can reach is a verdict nobody has read.
HB_DIR_SLOW_MS=${HB_DIR_SLOW_MS:-3000}
HB_STATIC="$(dirname "$HB")/.hb-static-control"
# ⭐ ENSURE THE CONTROL EXISTS ON EVERY RUN, NOT ON THE FIRST ALARM. Created lazily
#    inside the UNREADABLE branch, it would be born during the very firing it is
#    meant to explain -- with no cached attributes, hence unusable -- so the first
#    real alarm after deploy would answer "unsettled" and nothing would be learned
#    from it. Creating it here means it is already cold and quiet by the time
#    anything goes wrong, which is the whole job of a control.
# ⚠️ Cheap and quiet: one `test -e` per run, a create only if it is missing.
HB_STATIC_NEW=""
if ! timeout 10 test -e "$HB_STATIC" 2>/dev/null; then
  if : > "$HB_STATIC" 2>/dev/null; then HB_STATIC_NEW=new; else HB_STATIC_NEW=cannot; fi
fi
hb_static_control() {   # a file in $HB's directory that NOTHING rewrites
  case "$HB_STATIC_NEW" in
    # ⚠️ A FILE CREATED THIS RUN HAS NO CACHED ATTRIBUTES, so its first reading is
    #    not comparable to the heartbeat's. Say so rather than pass it off as fast.
    new)    echo "new new"; return ;;
    cannot) echo "- -"; return ;;
  esac
  mount_control "$HB_STATIC"
}

if ! timeout 25 test -f "$HB"; then
  read -r CTL_MS CTL_RC <<<"$(mount_control "$(dirname "$HB")")"
  read -r ROOT_MS ROOT_RC <<<"$(mount_control "$(hb_mount_root)")"
  read -r STAT_MS STAT_RC <<<"$(hb_static_control)"
  echo "$(stamp) UNREADABLE: $HB did not answer in 25s (controls: static sibling ${STAT_MS}ms rc=${STAT_RC}, its own directory ${CTL_MS}ms rc=${CTL_RC}, mount root $(hb_mount_root) ${ROOT_MS}ms rc=${ROOT_RC})"
  if [ "$CTL_RC" != "0" ] || [ "$ROOT_RC" != "0" ]; then
    VERDICT="=> THE MOUNT ITSELF IS NOT ANSWERING from $(hostname): its directory rc=${CTL_RC} in ${CTL_MS}ms, mount root $(hb_mount_root) rc=${ROOT_RC} in ${ROOT_MS}ms. This is an NFS/NAS problem, not a memo problem. Route it to the NAS owner, and do NOT read it as evidence about whether memo-watch is running -- nothing here can see that."
  elif [ "$STAT_RC" = "new" ]; then
    VERDICT="=> THE MOUNT IS HEALTHY (its directory ${CTL_MS}ms, mount root ${ROOT_MS}ms) but THE FILE-VS-DIRECTORY QUESTION IS UNSETTLED THIS RUN: the static sibling control did not exist and was created at the start of this run, so it has no cached attributes and its reading would not be comparable. The next firing will settle it. Not an NFS outage, and not evidence the watch is dead."
  elif [ "$STAT_RC" = "-" ]; then
    VERDICT="=> THE MOUNT IS HEALTHY (its directory ${CTL_MS}ms, mount root ${ROOT_MS}ms) but the static sibling control COULD NOT BE CREATED at $HB_STATIC from $(hostname), so file-vs-directory is unsettled. Check write permission on that directory. Not an NFS outage, and not evidence the watch is dead."
  elif [ "$STAT_RC" = "0" ] && [ "$STAT_MS" -ge "$HB_DIR_SLOW_MS" ]; then
    VERDICT="=> THE MOUNT IS HEALTHY BUT THIS DIRECTORY IS SLOW, NOT JUST THIS FILE. A static sibling that nothing rewrites took ${STAT_MS}ms in the same directory, while the mount root answered in ${ROOT_MS}ms. That points at churn on the directory rather than on the heartbeat inode: run replaces the heartbeat by an atomic rename every cadence, and a rename is a directory mutation, which is the expensive operation class on this mount (measured 2026-10-22: CREATE+UNLINK p50 28,470ms against SETATTR 472ms). Still not an NFS outage and still not evidence the watch is dead."
  else
    VERDICT="=> THE MOUNT IS HEALTHY AND SO IS THE DIRECTORY. A static sibling in the SAME directory answered in ${STAT_MS}ms and the mount root $(hb_mount_root) in ${ROOT_MS}ms, while $HB did not answer in 25s -- so the slow thing is this ONE FILE, not the mount and not the directory. Not an NFS outage, and not evidence the watch is dead. Measured 2026-10-22: the same pattern showed 9,160ms on the heartbeat against 92ms on a static file.
=> NOT ACTIONABLE BY THE NAS OWNER, AND NOT A PARTIAL-WRITE PROBLEM: run already writes the heartbeat to a temp name and renames it into place atomically (scripts/memo-watch/run:397), so a reader never sees a half-written file.
